comments.text:
@Philip Quaglino The marketing on the DLSS is really vague and potentially misleading. It kind of sound like you might have misunderstood it too from what you said. DLSS isn't going to make the card run games faster. It's just going to run games faster with DLSS COMPARED TO using TAA (and we have no information about what sort of TAA they used in those graphs and what load it presents). No matter how much run DLSS on dedicated hardware it's not going to be faster compared to not using any AA. The graphs from Nvidia sort of make it seem at a glance like it's some magic feature that will just run the games faster than normal, and that's just not going to happen.

This is exactly what makes me so skeptical - because TAA in modern games is typically quite resource-light, maybe 5% or 10% performance impact from my experience, not 25%+ (at least at 1080p, I don't really know about 4K since I don't run 4K). DLSS can't perform AA at less than a 0% performance impact obviously, so I suspect Nvidia used an unnecessarily heavy TAA in their testing to exaggerate the difference - and I fear that those results aren't very representative of real-world scenarios.

Aside from speed concerns I still suspect DLSS will be superior in image quality however and probably produce great quality without any blurring issues that is sometimes noticeable with TAA, so it's probably going to be a very nice feature regardless as long as software developers widely adopt it (and I don't see why not). I just suspect they are up-selling it a lot and giving false expectations to the consumers. Very much looking forward to some real test results.
